1. Evironment generation
Tiled plane generation seems to improve efficiency.

Random vegetation vs tile sampling.
On a word of same size (100 by 100), it is apparent that random environment runs much smoother with comparable density of forest. On sampled tile environment, the ROS clock slows down significantly.
I could see that 1 second of wall time equates to 2-3 seconds of ros time - slow sensing and navigation.

World size and density
The most functional configuration seems to be on the worls with 300 trees (200 oak, 100 pine) on a 100*100 plane. Using more trees noticeably slows down ROS. Scaling the plane up to 200*200
also slows down ROS, but not too much, it's still functional. However, bigger map requires more vegetation, which as mentioned before slows ROS significantly. With only 300 trees, the forest is very thin, and laser does not see anything, hence robot does not where to go, as most of the environment remains as "unknown cells" (robot does not know if its free space or there are obstacles). Might be resolved. Read further.

2. Navigation
Nav2 seems a bit odd. Oftentimes robot would stop and look around in wait, then proceed to goal. Occasionally abort the goal, although it is not stuck and there is a path to the goal.

Now, in thick forest robot seems to struggle less, as it sees trees and marks all the points towards it as empty cells on occupancy grid.

Another issue, for nav2 if goal lies outside of global costmap, robot will not navigate towards it. It basically does not know that there is space existing there. Global costmap is dynamic, and it expands when robot aproaches its boundaries and detects anything with lidar beyond the boundary.

3. Possible solutions
World generation - not sure how it can be improved. I do not have anymore ideas at this stage. We can work with 100 (maybe 150) sq metres map at this stage and may need to settle on that size. I think more research towards what slows ros down should be done.
I've tried playing with nav2 mapping, scaling down the resolution, but it does not impact much. I scaled global costmap to 0.2 resolution already, and thin downscaling further might make it troublesome for nav2

Navigation - now here I have two ideas

Solution one - tweak lidar. We might want to scale down the range of lidar when navigating (not patrolling the area), for robot to see less around it. And then, use all the invalid readings (when lidar beam does not reach anything) and define the cells in the beam's direction as empty ones. I can brief it as confident robot with poor eyesight. When it approaches an obstacle close enough (lets say 15-20m), it'll be able to see it and confidently mark the cells as occupied on the global costmap.

Solution two - supply robot with "map template" that will be of known dimentions of our world and have all cells marked as empty. This way, nav2 with run smoothly, as robot will be confident in cells around it that are free. It will also enable us to place goals for nav2 far away, where no detection has been done yet. However, it might be risky in terms of robot bumping into obstacles. As we mark all the cells free, it would mean that robot will lie its path through obstacles. So, unless SLAM can detect those and update global costmap fast enough, robot will hit an obstacle and possibly get stuck.


